  "rewardData": "Sac of cement__$10.00(https://cdn.images.indiegogo.com/productimage/noimage.jpg) | Glass of Water__$20.00(https://cdn.images.indiegogo.com/productimage/noimage.jpg) | Sanitization and hygiene__$30.00(https://cdn.images.indiegogo.com/productimage/noimage.jpg) | Shower__$50.00(https://cdn.images.indiegogo.com/productimage/noimage.jpg) | A card from the community__$100.00(https://cdn.images.indiegogo.com/productimage/noimage.jpg) | ",
  "tagTexts": "other | ",
  "shortDescription": "Help building an urgently needed well to provide safe drinking water.",
  "projectStory": "Project story\nCreator\nitzhakpreiss\nmore infocontact\nCategory\nHealth & Fitness\nTags\nother\nBacking information\nCrowdfunding risks\nlearn more\nOur Project: A community well\nClick on the photo to see a great video telling the story of water.\n\nAfter years of civil war, Liberians still struggle to gain access to drinking water, making everyday life extremely difficult. And with the government low on resources many communities seek to tackle the problem themselves.The PBS report above briefly explains some of the obstacles to fresh water encountered by many, and with the current Ebola outbreak and state of emergency declared in Monrovia, the country's capital, water has become even more scarce. \n\nTo solve this situation, the community in Barnesville, a short distance from central Monrovia, has mobilized and is organizing to build a water pump. The building and maintenance of the pump, done by the people of the community, will benefit all those living in the area, as there is currently no well. But in order to realize this essential goal, financial support is needed for the equipment, transportation and construction of the well. \n\nTo get water, people must walk into neighboring areas. With the restriction of movement due to the Ebola outbreak there are fewer and fewer options available. In addition to the distance and scarcity of water, there are also cases of well poisoning in wells that are not guarded, making drinking potentially life-threatening. This frustrating reality makes safe drinking water, which is a basic necessity, very hard to come by. A water pump owned and looked after by the community will alleviate the problem and empower the community, allowing us to address other problems and go on with their lives as healthier and happier people. For this, we kindly ask for your contribution! \n\n\n\nA Personal Story\nOmri Preiss, at the time a student living in the Netherlands, traveled to Ghana in 2006 to volunteer in Budubrum Refugee  Camp, home to a community of Liberians who had escaped the Civil Wars of the 1990s. There he met and worked with Emmanuel J. Toe, a teacher at the local primary school, and a Liberian refugee himself. Emmanuel was, and still is, a truly inspiring educator, who encouraged students to see themselves as the future of Liberia, with a responsibility to building a brighter future for their community. His vision, optimism and conviction were truly empowering. Omri and Emmanuel became friends, and stayed in touch in the years that followed. Emmanuel completed a business management diploma in Ghana, returned to Liberia together with many from Buduburam, and made a new home for his family in Barnesville. He continued to teach at the primary school there, while pursuing his own education, and working for his community.\nNow, with our connection between Liberia and the Netherlands, we are trying to support Emmanuel's community in their efforts to rebuild their lives and achieve a higher quality of life together. This is a simple personal intitive, there are no big NGOs involved, and contributions you make will go directly to the community, entirely for the purpose of safe cleaning water. Please get in touch with us if you have any questions!    \n\n\n\nThe Financial Side\nThe majority of the money will go towards paying for the pump. The remainder is used for materials and equipment, transportation and construction. The whole process is overseen by trusted and known community workers, ensuring that all the money raised is directly and transparently invested in the project. \nThe total cost of the project is $1,800. The following is a detailed breakdown of where the money will go: \nThe pump head - $800\n10 bags of cement, $10 each - $100\nWater pump machine to clean the well from dirty water - $100\n12 covers to be placed in the well, $50 per cover - $600\nTransportation from Monrovia to our community - $75\nWorkmanship and construction - $125\n The community will use its own resources for the project, and continue to raise money to ensure the completion of the project.  Money is an important means to reach many social goals, so any excess money will be used for equally important purposes if that should be the case!\n\n\nContribute and Share!\nWhile there is currently little that we can offer in return for your generous help, we will be extremely grateful and appreciative of all who make a contribution for our cause. As you well know, water is vital for everyone, and you can directly contribute to the well-being of an entire community of people! This is something that will be felt by everyone in the community and for large donations we will send a personal card with pictures of the well once it is finished, as a token of our gratitude. \n\nYour contributions will be greatly valued, but we understand that not everyone can contribute. If not, but still want to help us, let others know of our project and spread the word to individuals and organization who might be willing to help. In that way, you are also helping us make a change for the better!",
